module Formatter.Render.FlowPolicy exposing
    ( BlockKind(..)
    , ContainerSlot(..)
    , Decision
    , FlowConfig
    , FlowStart(..)
    , FlowState
    , ItemFacts(..)
    , NextSeparator(..)
    , Placement(..)
    , containerCommentSlot
    , decide
    , initialFlowState
    )

{-| The **gluing** flow's decision layer, consumed by the Box renderer.

The Box renderer's flow fold (`assembleFlow`) walks a sequence of sibling
LPNodes and joins every item onto the flow built so far. *How* an item joins
— glued with a space, dropped to its own line, wrapped as an indented block,
… — is a layout decision whose reparse fixed-point-ness the idempotency and
whitespace fuzzers have proven. This module holds that decision procedure,
kept out of the renderer so those decisions live in one place (matching
elm-format's ElmStructure layout rules) instead of being scattered through
the renderer's own state machine.

The invariant: **the renderer has no layout policy of its own.** Any decision
input richer than what `ItemFacts` carries (real source rows where this layer
tracks `-1`, rendered-box shape where it dispatches on box type) is not extra
precision — it is a divergence generator. A gluing flow's join decisions go
through `decide`; the renderer keeps only *materialization* (turning a
`Placement` into a `Box`).

**This is not the only policy layer, and reading it as one will send you to the
wrong file.** A flow the caller has already committed to breaking does not come
through here at all: `FlowAssembly.assembleBrokenWithComments` is the policy for
that case — where a comment glues backward onto the previous item's last line,
where it rides the next term's row, and the all-or-nothing run poisoning that
keeps a run from half-jumping ahead of itself. Broken calls, comment-bearing
binop chains and a pipeline step's stacked trailer route through it, and it
never calls `decide`. The two carry the same *classification* of a comment (the
stored `CommentRole`, `commentGluesToPrevious`, `pairableCommentLine`) and
deliberately differ in assembly; the migration that would have unified them was
stopped, and its dead `FlowConfig.brokenFlow` flag deleted, on the finding that
the assemblies need to differ. If a comment is landing in the wrong place, first
work out which of the two placed it.

One mirror of this module lives outside it and is stated here so it can be found
from this end: `NodeClassify.commentBreaksFlowRow` predicts, before anything is
rendered, which comments `decide` will let a following item share a row with. It
exists because `forceVertical` is derived from AST rows that have no comments in
them. Its own docstring says it must track `decide`;
`tests/audit-predicates.py` is what checks that it still does, by comparing it
against the flow this module actually assembles.

`decide` is total: the one error case in this area — a paired "comment" that
is not actually a block comment — is a caller-side classification error,
surfaced before facts are built.

@docs NextSeparator, FlowState, initialFlowState, FlowConfig, FlowStart, BlockKind
@docs ItemFacts, Placement, Decision, decide
@docs ContainerSlot, containerCommentSlot

-}

import Formatter.Logical.LogicalPrintingTree exposing
    ( CommentRole(..)
    , LPBox(..)
    , LPNode
    , ListBrackets(..)
    , commentGluesToPrevious
    , commentRole
    , lpnBox
    , lpnChildren
    , roleGlues
    )


{-| What separator to place before the next item.

  - `FirstItem` — nothing precedes; emit no separator.
  - `FlowSep` — normal flow: space or newline (a groupable soft break).
  - `HardNl` — after an `IndentedBlock`: forced newline.
  - `AlreadyTerminated` — after a `--`, or a single-line own-line comment, has
    ended its own line: no extra separator for a following TOKEN. A following
    COMMENT glues back onto that line iff its role says the author wrote it
    there, which after a `--` it never can (a `--` runs to EOL, so a comment
    written after one on the same row is inside its text).
  - `TerminatedByBlockComment` — after a *multi-line* block comment has ended
    its line: like `AlreadyTerminated`, except a same-row trailing comment (role
    `TrailsPrevious`/`RidesInline`) DOES glue onto that comment's closing
    delimiter line rather than dropping to its own. Kept distinct from
    `AlreadyTerminated` so a comment after a `--` / single-line comment still
    never glues.
  - `TerminatedByOwnLineComment` — after a single-line `{- … -}` that OPENED its
    own line inside a flow that was already hard-broken. The line holds nothing
    but comments, so a following **token** must start a fresh row
    (`joinPlacement` gives `OwnLine`).

    A following **comment** used to be allowed to merge up onto it, which was
    this state's original reason to exist. It no longer does: **gren never moves
    a comment between rows**, so a run the author wrote on separate rows keeps
    them (2026-08-08, a deliberate divergence — elm-format joins such a run).
    What survives is the TOKEN half, and it is load-bearing:

      - Answering `FlowSep` (what this arm did until 2026-08-06) space-joins the
        next TOKEN onto the comment's row. Where that token is a call whose
        arguments are already broken below it, gluing shifts the function right
        while its arguments keep their column — and a call's arguments must be
        indented past the function token, so `\item ->` ⏎ `{- c -} fn` ⏎ `arg`
        REPARSES as `(\item -> fn) arg`: an AST change, caught by `--show`'s AST
        comparison rather than by any idempotency check.
      - `AlreadyTerminated` would answer `GlueNoSep` for that token instead, and
        `HardNl` is what a real block leaves; neither says "the row above holds
        only comments", which is the fact this state carries.

    It propagates: once the row is a comment row, it stays one until something
    opens a new line.

-}
type NextSeparator
    = FirstItem
    | FlowSep
    | HardNl
    | AlreadyTerminated
    | TerminatedByBlockComment
    | TerminatedByOwnLineComment


{-| The decision state threaded through a flow fold.

  - `separator` — the join to apply *before* the next item (space, forced
    newline, already-terminated, …); see `NextSeparator`.

Comment placement no longer needs any source-row state here: whether a comment
glues onto the previous item or leads its own line is the stored `CommentRole`,
decided once in `Formatter.Logical.Comments` (see `CommentRole`'s docstring in
`Formatter.Logical.LogicalPrintingTree`). The old
`prevRowBlock` / `prevRowLine` / `prevElided` two-row state machine that
re-derived that at render time is gone.
-}
type alias FlowState =
    { separator : NextSeparator }


initialFlowState : FlowState
initialFlowState =
    { separator = FirstItem }


{-| Does the flow's first item continue a line the caller already started?

  - `InlineStart` — it does (a binop RHS, a `( … )` body), so a leading
    single-line block comment stays inline instead of standing on its own line.
  - `OwnStart` — it does not; the flow begins its own line.

A named type rather than a `Bool` because it is passed positionally beside an
indent at ~26 call sites, where `False 0` and `True 0` are indistinguishable
at a glance — one such copy-paste shipped a real indent bug (a record field
holding a lambda dropped its body 2 past the `{` instead of 4, fixed
2026-08-01). A `Bool` can be copied to the wrong site silently; this cannot.
-}
type FlowStart
    = InlineStart
    | OwnStart


{-| Per-flow configuration, fixed for the whole fold.

  - `indent` — the flow's own indent (the fold's base indent); the
    type-record drop uses it to pick the drop rule (`IndentedRule` when the
    flow is unnested, `BodyRule` when the caller already indented it).
  - `start` — see `FlowStart`.

-}
type alias FlowConfig =
    { indent : Int
    , start : FlowStart
    }


{-| Which block layout rule a block-style item joins with. The Box renderer
maps these to its wrap materializations: an indented hard break, a bare hard
break, and a soft indented break, respectively.
-}
type BlockKind
    = IndentedRule
    | BodyRule
    | SoftIndentedRule


{-| Everything `decide` is allowed to know about an item — the complete legal
input set; anything not listed here is banned as a decision input.

Renderer-supplied facts (`forcesVertical`, `childForcesVertical`) are computed
by the caller from the rendered box shape (`not isSingleLine`) — whether the
child ended up multi-line.

A comment item carries its `CommentRole` (decided once in
`Formatter.Logical.Comments`), which is all `decide` needs to place it — no
source rows. `LeafItem` no longer carries any facts: its only role is to advance
the separator state.
-}
type ItemFacts
    = LeafItem
    | LineCommentItem { role : CommentRole }
    | BlockCommentItem { multiLine : Bool, role : CommentRole }
    | BracketLiteralItem { forcesVertical : Bool, isTypeRecord : Bool }
    | IndentedBlockItem
    | BodyBlockItem
    | SoftBlockItem { childForcesVertical : Bool }
    | WhenBranchItem
    | PipelineStepItem
    | EmptyLineItem


{-| The fully-resolved join shape for one item — the complete set of join
shapes the flow fold's arms produce. `decide` resolves all separator-state
branching (`FirstItem` / `AlreadyTerminated` bypasses, same-row glue tests),
so a materializer is a straight `Placement -> combinator` map with no
decisions of its own.

  - `AsFirst` — the item stands alone (nothing precedes).
  - `GlueNoSep` — direct concatenation (after a comment has ended its own line).
  - `GlueSpace` — glue with a single space (same-row comment, `FlowSep`'s
    flat join).
  - `SoftSep` — the normal flow separator: a groupable soft break.
  - `OwnLine` — a forced newline before the item.
  - `OwnLineAfterBlank` — blank line, then the item on its own line
    (when-branch separation).
  - `IndentNoBreak` — the `AlreadyTerminated` non-bypass block join: the
    previous item (a comment) already ended its line, so indent the item to
    the wrap's level *without* a leading break (a literal space run pushes
    the first line there; a leading break would emit a whitespace-only blank
    line). Both non-bypass rules nest by exactly one indent step.
  - `BlockJoin` — the block wrap for `rule`.
  - `DropBlock` — the type-record drop: the item (bundled with its paired
    leading comment, if any — the caller assembles that) drops below its
    head via `rule`'s wrap. Only ever decided when the separator is
    `FlowSep`, so no bypass branches apply.
  - `BlankLineOnly` — emit one raw blank line; the item itself renders
    nothing (`EmptyLine` markers between `let` bindings).

-}
type Placement
    = AsFirst
    | GlueNoSep
    | GlueSpace
    | SoftSep
    | OwnLine
    | OwnLineAfterBlank
    | IndentNoBreak
    | BlockJoin { rule : BlockKind }
    | DropBlock BlockKind
    | BlankLineOnly


type alias Decision =
    { placement : Placement
    , next : FlowState
    }


{-| The four slots a comment can take among a vertical container's children.
See `containerCommentSlot`. -}
type ContainerSlot
    = LeadsOpener
    | LeadsFollowing
    | GluesPrevious
    | StandsAlone


{-| Where a comment sits among the children of a VERTICAL container — a union
body's variants, a record update's fields, a `when`'s branches, a bracket
list's items.

This is the container-fold twin of `decide`. `decide` places an item in a
*flow*; this places a comment among *container children*, which is a different
question with a much smaller answer set. Each container then materializes the
answer its own way, because the materializations genuinely differ (a
when-branch stacks its lead run above the branch and separates branches with
blank lines; a bracket list glues the run onto the item's front behind a `, `
prefix; a union body has no prefix at all).

What must NOT differ is the classification, and before 2026-08-02 it was four
hand-written if-chains that agreed only by inspection — one of them said so:
the update fold's state "mirrors `commentBracketListBox`'s `pending`", an
invariant asserted in prose and enforced by nothing.

The caller supplies the two facts only it can know:

  - `atOpener` — nothing has been emitted yet AND this container has an opener
    slot, so a comment here was written just after the `[`/`{` and leads the
    first item. Containers with no opener slot (a union body, a when's
    branches) pass `False`; the record update's opener slot is peeled off
    before the fold (`splitOpenerComments`) so it passes `False` too.
  - `canGlue` — there is something this comment may glue onto. Each container
    defines that differently, and the differences are deliberate: the update
    requires the previous child to be a real FIELD (so two comments flanking a
    `|` each keep their own line instead of chaining), while the union body,
    the when-branches and the bracket list allow a second trailing comment to
    chain onto the first.

The result order is the priority order, and it is the same in all four.
-}
containerCommentSlot : { atOpener : Bool, canGlue : Bool } -> LPNode -> ContainerSlot
containerCommentSlot facts node =
    if facts.atOpener then
        LeadsOpener

    else if commentRole (lpnBox node) == Just LeadsNext then
        -- Rule C2: written across a separator the parser records no position
        -- for, so it belongs to the item that FOLLOWS it.
        LeadsFollowing

    else if facts.canGlue && commentGluesToPrevious node then
        GluesPrevious

    else
        StandsAlone


{-| THE decision procedure: how does this item join the flow, and what state
does the fold carry forward. Pure and total; the procedure the idempotency and
whitespace fuzzers have proven to be a reparse fixed point.

No arm here ever emits a blank line before an item. Blank lines separate
statements and declarations — top-level units, `let` bindings, `when` cases,
`if`/`else` branches — and those are placed by `Formatter.Logical.VerticalSpace`
as `EmptyLine` markers before the flow ever reaches this layer. Inside a single
expression — a pipeline, a binop chain, a list, a record — the parts are never
separated by a blank line, including an own-line comment leading a part.
-}
decide : FlowConfig -> FlowState -> ItemFacts -> Decision
decide config state facts =
    when facts is
        LeafItem ->
            { placement = joinPlacement state.separator
            , next = { separator = FlowSep }
            }

        LineCommentItem f ->
            -- A `--` comment on the same row as the preceding item (or one
            -- trailing an elided `->`) glues with a space; on a different
            -- row it forces an own-line break. Either way it ends its line
            -- with a forced newline (the materializer appends it), so the next
            -- item starts fresh: AlreadyTerminated. A line comment reads
            -- `prevRowLine`, not `prevRowBlock`: it is unambiguously trailing (a `--`
            -- runs to EOL, nothing follows it on the row), so it glues onto the
            -- preceding item's last *token* row even where a block comment there
            -- stays own-line (a call `foo bar`, a lone `{}` / `[]` / single-line
            -- bracket — whose `prevRowBlock` is `-1` to keep block comments off-line,
            -- matching elm-format). This is what makes `foo bar -- c` /
            -- `{} -- c` a reparse fixed point instead of a +4 ↔ col-0 oscillation.
            { placement = commentPlacement config state f.role
            , next = { separator = AlreadyTerminated }
            }

        BlockCommentItem f ->
            let
                -- A block comment forces an own-line break (so the next
                -- token starts fresh at the right column) when it is
                -- multi-line, OR when it is a standalone leading comment
                -- in a vertical context — the first item of a flow that
                -- does not continue a line the caller already started
                -- (e.g. before a non-first `let` binding), OR when a
                -- previous comment already ended the line (AlreadyTerminated):
                -- a run of own-line comments must each stay on their own
                -- line, not glue the following token onto the last one.
                -- A single-line block comment that continues a line stays
                -- inline (`a * {- c -} b`, a binop RHS, a `( … )` body).
                standaloneLeading =
                    (state.separator == FirstItem && config.start == OwnStart)
                        || state.separator == AlreadyTerminated
                        || state.separator == TerminatedByBlockComment
            in
            if f.multiLine || standaloneLeading then
                let
                    placement =
                        commentPlacement config state f.role
                in
                { placement = placement
                , next =
                    -- A multi-line block comment always leaves the glueable
                    -- terminator (a same-row trailing comment rides onto its
                    -- `-}` line, whether this one itself glued or stands alone).
                    -- A single-line comment that GLUED onto a preceding line
                    -- (placement `GlueSpace`) leaves `HardNl`: a further
                    -- same-row comment still glues onto the same line (a 3+ run
                    -- like `{- multi⏎line -} {- a -} {- b -}` stays together —
                    -- `commentPlacement`'s `_` arm glues a `roleGlues` comment
                    -- after `HardNl`), but a following *token* is forced onto
                    -- its own line (`joinPlacement HardNl == OwnLine`) instead
                    -- of gluing onto the comment — the glued comment did NOT
                    -- terminate its line, so `FlowSep` would space-join the next
                    -- token onto it (`-} {- c -} second =`, which misparses).
                    -- A single-line comment that stood on its own line leaves
                    -- the plain terminator (a following comment stays own-line).
                    { separator =
                        if f.multiLine then
                            TerminatedByBlockComment

                        else if placement == GlueSpace then
                            HardNl

                        else
                            AlreadyTerminated
                    }
                }

            else
                -- Single-line block comment continuing a line: inline. A
                -- comment on the same source row as the previous item glues
                -- onto that item's last rendered line with a space. This
                -- matters after a *block* (a dropped type record, whose
                -- `prevRowBlock` is its `}` row): the HardNl separator would push
                -- the comment to a fresh line, which reparses as an own-line
                -- comment and detaches (a +4 ↔ col-0 oscillation). For an
                -- inline previous item the `SoftSep` join already renders a
                -- space, so this is a no-op there.
                let
                    placement =
                        -- Glue onto the current line only when there IS one: at
                        -- `FirstItem` nothing precedes, so fall through to
                        -- `joinPlacement` (which yields `AsFirst`).
                        if roleGlues f.role && state.separator /= FirstItem then
                            GlueSpace

                        else
                            -- A comment the author gave its own row keeps it,
                            -- including above a row that holds only comments:
                            -- `joinPlacement TerminatedByOwnLineComment` is
                            -- `OwnLine` and that is the answer for a comment as
                            -- well as for a token. Merging it up (this arm
                            -- answered `joinPlacement FlowSep` until 2026-08-08,
                            -- to match elm-format, which joins such a run) is
                            -- the one way gren moved a comment between rows that
                            -- the author had not asked for. The token half of
                            -- that state is untouched and still load-bearing —
                            -- see `NextSeparator`.
                            joinPlacement state.separator
                in
                { placement = placement
                , next =
                    -- Carry this comment's row forward (not -1) so a FURTHER
                    -- same-row trailing comment glues onto the same line too — a
                    -- run like `] {- a -} {- b -}` keeps all of them on the `]`
                    -- line instead of dropping the second to its own line (which
                    -- would detach on reparse).
                    --
                    -- A comment that GLUES onto a hard-broken line does not undo
                    -- the break: `HardNl` survives it. The arm above leaves
                    -- `HardNl` for a comment that glued onto a line a following
                    -- TOKEN must not join (`joinPlacement HardNl == OwnLine`),
                    -- and the SECOND member of a run reaches this arm instead —
                    -- `standaloneLeading` does not list `HardNl` — so answering
                    -- `FlowSep` there space-joined the next token onto the run:
                    --
                    --     {- standalone        the `-}` row already forces a
                    --        multi-line          break before a token, so with
                    --        comment -} {- a -}  ONE comment `second` goes below
                    --     second =                — and with TWO it came back up
                    --
                    -- which for a `let` binding is not merely wrong but
                    -- unparseable: a binding must start at the block's column.
                    -- An older note here said a following plain item was
                    -- unaffected because "plain items join by separator"; that is
                    -- the mechanism, not an exemption — `FlowSep` IS the
                    -- space-join.
                    --
                    -- Scoped to a comment that GLUED, and that scope is the whole
                    -- rule. A comment that OPENED its own line (placement
                    -- `OwnLine`) starts a fresh comment line and KEEPS it: a run
                    -- the author wrote on separate rows stays on separate rows.
                    -- Until 2026-08-08 a following own-line comment merged up
                    -- onto it, to match elm-format, which joins such a run
                    -- (`PipelineLambdaArgTrailingComment` pinned the merged
                    -- form). That is a deliberate divergence now: gren never
                    -- moves a comment between rows.
                    { separator =
                        if state.separator == HardNl && placement == GlueSpace then
                            HardNl

                        else if
                            placement == OwnLine
                                || state.separator == TerminatedByOwnLineComment
                        then
                            -- This comment opened a row of its own (or merged
                            -- onto one that did). The row is comments-only, and
                            -- stays so through any further merge — a following
                            -- TOKEN must not space-join onto it, which is what
                            -- `FlowSep` here used to do.
                            TerminatedByOwnLineComment

                        else
                            FlowSep
                    }
                }

        BracketLiteralItem f ->
            -- A multi-line *type* record that follows a real head in a flow
            -- DROPS to its own line at +indent — matching elm-format's
            -- application rule (`HasIdentifier\n    { … }`, `tracing :\n    { … }`)
            -- — instead of gluing its first line onto the head. Gates:
            --   • `isTypeRecord` — only type records, so expression flows
            --     (records / arrays / patterns) that share the fold stay inline.
            --   • separator == FlowSep — real content precedes (not FirstItem,
            --     a leading comment's AlreadyTerminated, or HardNl after a block).
            -- A `A -> … -> { record }` return record also drops (fixed-point
            -- since the signature's forceVertical folds in
            -- `segmentHasDroppingRecord`, committing to the fully-broken
            -- signature layout up front instead of oscillating on reparse).
            -- Drop rule lands the record at flow-base + one indent step:
            -- `IndentedRule` when the flow is unnested (`indent == 0`, a
            -- type-alias body / union variant), `BodyRule` (hardNl only) when
            -- the flow is already nested (a record-type field value).
            let
                followsRealHead =
                    state.separator == FlowSep
            in
            if followsRealHead && f.forcesVertical && f.isTypeRecord then
                let
                    rule =
                        if config.indent == 0 then
                            IndentedRule

                        else
                            BodyRule
                in
                -- Carry the record's closing-bracket row as `prevRowBlock` (not -1)
                -- so a single-line block comment on that same source row glues
                -- onto the record's `}` line (`} {- c -}`) instead of dropping
                -- to a fresh line — which would reparse as an own-line comment
                -- and detach (a +4 ↔ col-0 oscillation). Keeping the rule's
                -- `nextSep` (HardNl / FlowSep) means a following *type node*
                -- still breaks to its own line; only a same-row comment glues
                -- (see the BlockCommentItem inline arm's same-row path).
                { placement = DropBlock rule
                , next = { separator = blockNextSep rule }
                }

            else if f.forcesVertical then
                -- A multi-line EXPRESSION bracket (list / record / update as a
                -- value). Like the type-record path above, carry its closing-
                -- bracket row as `prevRowBlock` so a same-row trailing comment glues
                -- onto the `]`/`}` line (`] {- c -}`) — a reparse fixed point —
                -- instead of dropping to its own line at the body indent, which
                -- reparses as an own-line comment and detaches (the +4 ↔ col-0
                -- oscillation). A following non-comment item still breaks
                -- (`separator = FlowSep`); only a same-row comment glues.
                { placement = joinPlacement state.separator
                , next = { separator = FlowSep }
                }

            else
                -- A single-line EXPRESSION bracket (`{ a = 1 }`, `[ 1, 2 ]`).
                -- A same-row trailing `--` line comment glues onto the `}`/`]`
                -- line (`prevRowLine = closingRow`) instead of dropping to its own
                -- line, which reparses as an own-line comment and detaches (the
                -- +4 ↔ col-0 oscillation). A *block* comment keeps `prevRowBlock = -1`
                -- so it stays own-line — matching elm-format and the canonical
                -- comment fixtures, which put a block comment after a single-line
                -- bracket (`{ x } {- … -}`) on its own line. A following
                -- non-comment item still joins by separator.
                { placement = joinPlacement state.separator
                , next = { separator = FlowSep }
                }

        IndentedBlockItem ->
            blockDecision IndentedRule state

        BodyBlockItem ->
            blockDecision BodyRule state

        SoftBlockItem f ->
            blockDecision
                (if f.childForcesVertical then
                    IndentedRule

                 else
                    SoftIndentedRule
                )
                state

        WhenBranchItem ->
            -- UNREACHABLE: `factsFor` never constructs a `WhenBranchItem` —
            -- `WhenBranch` nodes route through the dedicated
            -- `renderWhenBranchesBox`/`makeWhenBranchBox` path, never through
            -- this generic flow assembler. MakeRenderBox's `OwnLineAfterBlank`
            -- materializer arm is the other half of the proof: it Errs, and
            -- this arm is the only thing that could produce that placement.
            --
            -- It stays a real policy rather than the `Err "unreachable: …"`
            -- idiom used elsewhere because `decide` is total by design — it
            -- returns a `Decision`, not a `Result`, so this arm has no way to
            -- fail. Kept for the item kind in case the routing ever changes.
            --
            -- Intent, if it did fire: always blank-separate consecutive
            -- branches, but attach directly (GlueNoSep) after a preceding
            -- comment (which already ended its line) to avoid an extra blank
            -- between the comment and its pattern.
            { placement =
                when state.separator is
                    FirstItem ->
                        AsFirst

                    AlreadyTerminated ->
                        GlueNoSep

                    _ ->
                        OwnLineAfterBlank
            , next = { separator = FlowSep }
            }

        PipelineStepItem ->
            -- A pipeline step stacks on its own line under the seed. An own-line
            -- comment leading a step stays glued to that step with no blank line
            -- above it: a pipeline is one expression, and blank lines never
            -- separate the parts of a single expression (see `decide`'s header).
            { placement =
                when state.separator is
                    FirstItem ->
                        AsFirst

                    _ ->
                        BlockJoin { rule = IndentedRule }
            , next = { separator = HardNl }
            }

        EmptyLineItem ->
            -- A blank-line marker between `let` bindings. Keep separator =
            -- HardNl so the following binding still wraps onto its own line.
            { placement = BlankLineOnly
            , next = { separator = HardNl }
            }


-- The plain-item join: how a non-comment, non-block item follows the pending
-- separator.
joinPlacement : NextSeparator -> Placement
joinPlacement sep =
    when sep is
        FirstItem ->
            AsFirst

        FlowSep ->
            SoftSep

        HardNl ->
            OwnLine

        AlreadyTerminated ->
            GlueNoSep

        -- A non-comment item after a comment-terminated line glues directly,
        -- same as `AlreadyTerminated` (only a following *comment* treats the
        -- two states differently — see `commentPlacement`).
        TerminatedByBlockComment ->
            GlueNoSep

        -- The row holds only comments, so a TOKEN starts a fresh one. (A
        -- following comment does not come through here — the comment arm treats
        -- this state as `FlowSep` so the run can still merge.)
        TerminatedByOwnLineComment ->
            OwnLine


{-| A comment glues onto the previous item's line when the classifier tagged it
`TrailsPrevious` (a `--` or `{- -}` that trails the item) or `RidesInline`
(an inline `{- -}` riding a flat flow). `LeadsOwnLine` / `Standalone` do not
glue here.
-}
-- The own-line-comment join: a `TrailsPrevious` / `RidesInline` comment glues
-- with a space; a `LeadsOwnLine` comment forces its own line. The glue/own-line
-- call is the stored `CommentRole` — decided once in `Comments.gren` from the
-- pristine parse rows — not a render-time row comparison. `config` is unused
-- today but keeps the helper's signature uniform with the arms.
commentPlacement : FlowConfig -> FlowState -> CommentRole -> Placement
commentPlacement _ state role =
    when state.separator is
        FirstItem ->
            AsFirst

        -- After a `--`, or a single-line comment that took its own line: a
        -- same-row trailing comment glues onto it, an own-line one stays
        -- own-line. Role-aware for the same reason the arm below is — **gren
        -- never moves a comment between rows**, so a member the author wrote on
        -- the previous member's row shares that row here too. Answering
        -- `GlueNoSep` unconditionally (as this did until 2026-08-08) split every
        -- authored one-row run into one row per member.
        --
        -- A `--` also reaches this state, and needs no exception: it runs to end
        -- of line, so a comment written after one on the SAME row is inside its
        -- text and never becomes a node. Anything the classifier can hand us
        -- after a `--` was written on a later row, and its role says so.
        AlreadyTerminated ->
            if roleGlues role then
                GlueSpace

            else
                GlueNoSep

        -- After a multi-line block comment: a same-row trailing comment glues
        -- onto its `-}` line; an own-line one stays own-line (`GlueNoSep`
        -- materializes as its own line, like `AlreadyTerminated`).
        TerminatedByBlockComment ->
            if roleGlues role then
                GlueSpace

            else
                GlueNoSep

        _ ->
            if roleGlues role then
                GlueSpace

            else
                OwnLine


-- The block-style join: FirstItem stands alone; after AlreadyTerminated the
-- BodyRule (whose wrap begins with its own forced newline) glues directly,
-- while the two nesting rules take the no-leading-break
-- indent path (see `IndentNoBreak`); otherwise the rule's wrap applies.
blockDecision : BlockKind -> FlowState -> Decision
blockDecision rule state =
    { placement =
        when state.separator is
            FirstItem ->
                AsFirst

            AlreadyTerminated ->
                if rule == BodyRule then
                    GlueNoSep

                else
                    IndentNoBreak

            -- Same as `AlreadyTerminated`: a block item after a comment-ended
            -- line indents without a leading break.
            TerminatedByBlockComment ->
                if rule == BodyRule then
                    GlueNoSep

                else
                    IndentNoBreak

            _ ->
                BlockJoin { rule = rule }
    , next = { separator = blockNextSep rule }
    }


-- The separator state a block-style item leaves behind: an IndentedRule
-- block forces the next item onto its own line; the other two flow on.
blockNextSep : BlockKind -> NextSeparator
blockNextSep rule =
    when rule is
        IndentedRule ->
            HardNl

        BodyRule ->
            FlowSep

        SoftIndentedRule ->
            FlowSep


-- ═══ STRUCTURAL CLASSIFIERS (renderer-neutral, LPNode-based) ═══


-- True if the node is a line or block comment. (Doc comments are their own
-- top-level OriginalRows so they don't appear in mid-flow positions.)
